WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:04.080
You're looking at this and going, "Wow!&nbsp; Wouldn't it be amazing if this worked."

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.240
And then you realize, "Oh my gosh, it does work!"

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.960
National borders are just speed bumps&nbsp;
in the information superhighway.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:14.419
Cryptography is really, really strong.&nbsp;We can do whatever we want here.

00:00:14.419 --> 00:00:18.480
It might provide the equivalent of what&nbsp;government does in a decentralized fashion.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.527
I talk about collapse of governments. I think&nbsp;we actually saw that in the Middle Ages,

00:00:22.527 --> 00:00:27.040
probably as a result of the printing press.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.080
This is the story of a community of scientists,&nbsp;mathematicians, philosophers, and activists

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.400
who believe that the internet combined with recent&nbsp;breakthroughs in the field of cryptography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.680
would upend society and usher in a&nbsp;new era of sovereign individualism.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.355
We have it in our power to begin&nbsp;the world over again right now.

00:00:45.355 --> 00:00:49.405
And its internal clash over the&nbsp;optimal path to achieving that goal.

00:00:49.405 --> 00:00:53.440
Can evil be done with this technology? Yes.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.440
And so, what?

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.560
Deal with it.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.579
Here's the final installment&nbsp;in a four-part series

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:06.994
on the origins of the cypherpunk movement and&nbsp;how it helped shape our modern world.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:26.234
We see a free world that has&nbsp;achieved a level of prosperity unprecedented in all human history. 

00:01:26.234 --> 00:01:32.492
Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall!

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:38.945
If you had asked me 10 years&nbsp;ago, "is it possible that&nbsp;communism could collapse by the year 1992,"

00:01:38.945 --> 00:01:42.175
I would've said, "absolutely ridiculous."

00:01:42.175 --> 00:01:46.274
The fall of the Berlin Wall was important to me&nbsp;because I thought it was the end of history

00:01:46.274 --> 00:01:52.793
and that national borders would cease being the walls&nbsp;of prisons.

00:01:52.793 --> 00:01:55.733
And so, a couple years later, when I discovered the internet, I thought,

00:01:55.733 --> 00:02:04.171
"Oh, this is part of this pattern where borders and distance stop being barriers to people." 

00:02:04.171 --> 00:02:11.144
Then I discovered&nbsp;a whole clan of people who self-identified as&nbsp;computer people. 

00:02:11.144 --> 00:02:17.251
I found my tribe. And among&nbsp;that clan was the cypherpunks mailing list.

00:02:17.251 --> 00:02:21.840
If you want to subscribe to our mailing list,&nbsp;send a blank email message and just say subscribe cypherpunks.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.406
And what is a cypherpunk?&nbsp;What do you guys stand for? Cypherpunks are cryptography activists.

00:02:26.406 --> 00:02:30.320
The cypherpunks email list, which&nbsp;started in the fall of 1992,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.760
one year after the launch of the World Wide Web,&nbsp;became a gathering place for a global community

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:41.609
interested in using cryptography to allow&nbsp;individuals to communicate and transact on the internet privately 

00:02:41.609 --> 00:02:44.261
and without&nbsp;interference from a central authority.

00:02:44.261 --> 00:02:51.913
In the last 20 years, the basic techniques&nbsp;to do all sorts of impossible-seeming things have suddenly emerged.

00:02:51.913 --> 00:02:53.600
Put them into practice.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.920
Many cypherpunks were inspired by the work&nbsp;of the computer scientist David Chaum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.185
who had demonstrated that it was possible&nbsp;to use cryptography

00:03:01.185 --> 00:03:04.871
to build an anonymous payment network that ran on the internet.

00:03:04.871 --> 00:03:09.364
Because of the cypherpunks and because of the&nbsp;science papers of David Chaum,

00:03:09.364 --> 00:03:12.763
I thought the third thing that's going to happen is economic freedom. 

00:03:12.763 --> 00:03:19.087
That people are going to be no longer constrained&nbsp;by national borders and distance 

00:03:19.087 --> 00:03:23.680
from cooperating&nbsp;and sharing resources and helping each other.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.309
But at this watershed moment of collapsing&nbsp;borders, there was a divide within the cypherpunk community

00:03:29.309 --> 00:03:33.440
over whether these cryptographic tools&nbsp;would lead to more individual freedom, free trade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.361
and the spread of democracy, or the end of&nbsp;
government altogether. 

00:03:37.361 --> 00:03:43.520
The cypherpunk movement's most influential figure was the physicist and&nbsp;intellectual provocateur Tim May,

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.424
who coined the phrase 'crypto anarchy.' 

00:03:45.424 --> 00:03:51.945
He saw the fall of the Berlin wall as evidence that the societal institutions we take for granted could collapse in short order,

00:03:51.945 --> 00:03:53.810
just as they had in the Middle Ages.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:57.735
We saw the little principalities,&nbsp;the monarchies, the religious,&nbsp;the papal states,

00:03:57.735 --> 00:04:02.587
we saw those collapse&nbsp;probably as a result of publishing, printing.

00:04:02.587 --> 00:04:07.167
May penned a one-page summary of how&nbsp; cryptography would upend society.

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:12.661
So I just sat down at my little&nbsp;Macintosh and loosely patterned&nbsp;this after the Communist Manifesto.

00:04:12.661 --> 00:04:17.418
"A specter is haunting the modern&nbsp;world, the specter of crypto-anarchy."

00:04:17.418 --> 00:04:25.120
Cryptography is really, really strong. We can be protected from anything without, from

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:33.875
the observers, from the watchers, from the imposers of&nbsp;the past, upon the future we're trying to create.

00:04:33.875 --> 00:04:40.545
May was skeptical of the idea that humanity was&nbsp;witnessing an unabashed victory of economic and political liberalism 

00:04:40.545 --> 00:04:44.000
or that it was possible&nbsp;to overcome tyranny through collective action.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.120
As the political scientist&nbsp;Francis Fukuyama argued,

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.407
"We as human beings want to be&nbsp;recognized with a certain dignity.

00:04:51.407 --> 00:04:54.960
This is the essential driving force&nbsp;behind democratic revolutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.040
whether they take place in Eastern&nbsp;Europe or in Asia or in Latin America."

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.607
May embraced a technology-based theory of historic&nbsp;change summed up by the movement's tagline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.607 --> 00:05:08.720
which was coined by the mathematician&nbsp;Eric Hughes, "cypherpunks write code."

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:14.484
What Eric meant by "cypherpunks write code" is don't be one of those guys who goes to a Libertarian Party conference

00:05:14.484 --> 00:05:18.938
and sits about&nbsp;getting somebody elected to the Los Gatos&nbsp;City Council. 

00:05:18.938 --> 00:05:22.240
That way lies madness.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:25.840
Whereas the interesting things that had&nbsp;happened had been technological changes,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.880
the telephone, copy machine, the VCR,

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.418
the thing that I really got interested in is that&nbsp;you don't just go and ask the regulator,

00:05:33.418 --> 00:05:40.498
"Oh, we need more privacy online.' We go fix it.&nbsp;Arguing and complaining and lobbying and politics have nominal effects.

00:05:40.498 --> 00:05:49.056
And what changes the&nbsp;world is technology adoption and society moving,&nbsp;shifting its viewpoints.

00:05:49.056 --> 00:05:54.719
For crypto-anarchists like May, writing code&nbsp;meant building systems for anonymous transactions on the internet.

00:05:54.719 --> 00:05:58.160
It made the arbitrary&nbsp;divisions of the political world irrelevant.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.840
National borders are just speed bumps&nbsp;on the information superhighway.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.520
Online cryptographic networks would&nbsp;be structured like a geodesic dome,

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:11.400
a form hailed by the counter-cultural&nbsp;technologists of the 1970s for being in&nbsp;harmony with nature

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.680
and highly resistant to external attack.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:19.549
Networks with no owners with many interconnecting&nbsp;nodes that would be basically unstoppable.

00:06:19.549 --> 00:06:23.867
In a geodesic market, "economics&nbsp;will no longer be the handmaiden&nbsp;of politics,"

00:06:23.867 --> 00:06:26.762
the cyberpunk writer&nbsp;Robert Hettinga noted. 

00:06:26.762 --> 00:06:31.760
"Nation-states will eventually be as ceremonial as&nbsp;modern-day constitutional monarchs."

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:36.926
This idea of a many-to-many connection&nbsp;
was clearly going to happen.

00:06:36.926 --> 00:06:40.974
But it was much easier to build a&nbsp;functional network for cooperation and trade

00:06:40.974 --> 00:06:44.240
when you can rely on a central authority to enforce the rules.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:51.200
How could we use this weird crypto technique to&nbsp;solve this strange, esoteric little problem that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.200
in the real world you just solve because, "Oh,&nbsp;I know what your social security number is,"&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:01.577
or you have to have your government-issued ID&nbsp;that you take to the bank so that you can open up your bank account,

00:07:01.577 --> 00:07:06.988
and they can link the two&nbsp;together and say, "Okay, if Jim McCoy walks out&nbsp;on this $100,000 loan,

00:07:06.988 --> 00:07:10.252
well, we know where to find&nbsp;him." It was trying to deal with those problems.

00:07:10.252 --> 00:07:14.080
You can buy and sell property in&nbsp;cyberspace using cryptographic protocols.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:20.683
The idea of ways in which people might&nbsp;provide the equivalent of what government&nbsp;does in a decentralized fashion.

00:07:20.683 --> 00:07:23.389
Tim&nbsp;stole some ideas from my Machinery of&nbsp;Freedom

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:27.113
and he reprocessed them with all this&nbsp;technological stuff, and then I stole them back.

00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:32.917
A function of government that was particularly&nbsp;hard to replicate using cryptography was the issuance of money.

00:07:32.917 --> 00:07:37.959
The cypherpunks attempted to&nbsp;build a borderless internet currency system as&nbsp;anonymous as cash,

00:07:37.959 --> 00:07:41.840
and that like gold, held its&nbsp;value without the backing of a central bank.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:48.211
Gold makes very good money&nbsp;because nobody can manufacture more of it very readily, to put it mildly. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.211 --> 00:07:54.800
Right? Whereas bits are perfectly copyable,&nbsp;&nbsp; so turning bits into good&nbsp;money is quite difficult.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:01.040
Tim May and many others considered&nbsp;electronic cash to be the holy grail&nbsp;because it completed the picture.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:04.480
A private and decentralized&nbsp;monetary system, May argued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:08.960
was a key component in constructing a new&nbsp;borderless world where the activities

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:13.760
and assets of individuals would be resistant&nbsp;to government control and confiscation.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:20.720
You don't physically meet the&nbsp;person, if you don't even know&nbsp;what continent they're on you can't coerce them.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:28.000
But there was another faction within&nbsp;the movement that rejected Tim May's&nbsp;vision of cordoning off a new world in cyberspace.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:33.280
The High-Tech Hayekians were people like&nbsp;
Eric Drexler, Mark Miller, Phil Salin.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:38.305
They were focused on designing secure computing&nbsp;systems based on economic insights,

00:08:38.305 --> 00:08:42.600
particularly those of the Austrian-born Nobel Prize winner&nbsp;Friedrich Hayek.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:46.804
Instead of building a new virtual world shielded from government interference, 

00:08:46.804 --> 00:08:53.964
the High-Tech Hayekians sought to use technology to demolish walls and divisions within the existing world. 

00:08:53.964 --> 00:08:57.615
They imagined that introducing new tools&nbsp;for human coordination

00:08:57.615 --> 00:09:01.520
would gradually erode the government's ability to impinge on our freedoms.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:11.280
We did lots and lots of fantasizing&nbsp;about how the world could be different,&nbsp;but we saw this as emerging from inside the world.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.640
Cryptography was a tool for porting economic&nbsp;concepts and legal structures onto the internet.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:21.200
But the aim was to foster new forms of&nbsp;peer-to-peer commerce and knowledge sharing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:28.160
And the High-Tech Hayekians believed that even&nbsp;imperfect systems can transform&nbsp;society gradually from within.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:35.280
The overall arc of world history is towards&nbsp;
rule of law, towards less corruptible systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:44.160
If the emergence of the world of crypto-commerce creates systems that are vastly less corruptible,

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:53.440
but under a whole mix of different&nbsp;mechanisms and governance regimes such that

00:09:53.440 --> 00:10:02.042
they're not always everywhere&nbsp;incorruptible, I think that's fine.

00:10:02.042 --> 00:10:09.836
There's a lot of power in providing people&nbsp;tools such that they can successfully start&nbsp;to act more like you would like them to.

00:10:09.836 --> 00:10:15.593
As opposed to, "You've got to come over here where it's really hard edge encrypted and&nbsp;it works exactly the way we want."

00:10:15.593 --> 00:10:22.240
No no, let's raise people's levels incrementally,&nbsp;and that's an improvement in the world.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:27.179
For many cypherpunks the darker side of crypto-anarchy was epitomized by the writings

00:10:27.179 --> 00:10:31.710
of a chemist and electrical engineer Jim Bell,&nbsp;a participant on the email list,

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:37.440
who in the mid-nineties compared aggression by the state to&nbsp;that of muggers, rapists, robbers, and murderers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:43.361
and posited a cryptographically protected&nbsp;marketplace in which anonymous individuals could in effect,

00:10:43.361 --> 00:10:47.760
pay to have government employees&nbsp;killed with the goal of destroying the state.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.078
In 1997, Bell was arrested and went&nbsp;to prison for, among other things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.078 --> 00:10:54.818
dropping a stink bomb on a government building.

00:10:54.818 --> 00:11:01.490
Tim May distanced himself from Bell's writings and activities while maintaining&nbsp;that marketplaces for assassination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:06.160
like the one Bell had described, might&nbsp;be both inevitable and desirable.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:16.157
Can evil be done with&nbsp;this technology? Not just the internet, but especially the crypto part of it? Yes.

00:11:16.157 --> 00:11:18.160
And so what?

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:20.500
Deal with it.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:27.206
Zimmerman once told me that&nbsp;he sometimes regretted ever introducing PGP to the world

00:11:27.206 --> 00:11:35.109
because it could be used&nbsp;by Al-Qaeda or the Taliban or whatnot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.109 --> 00:11:40.709
I say, so what? I'm not morally responsible.

00:11:40.709 --> 00:11:46.071
It would have happened whether we had existed or not. 

00:11:46.071 --> 00:11:48.720
Because technology has its own logic?

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:54.000
And people want to do what they&nbsp;want to do. I mean, they want free sh*t.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:02.149
They want freedom to do things. Even if they say that it should be regulated, they'll often make the conscious decision to copy music, 

00:12:02.149 --> 00:12:04.000
copy videotapes they want to see.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:08.606
BlackNet is a negative consequence. I'm not&nbsp;
interested in creating that.

00:12:08.606 --> 00:12:15.494
When you build technology to solve problems, then you create— then there are consequences that we try to think ahead of, 

00:12:15.494 --> 00:12:18.456
and look at, and figure out how we might address, 

00:12:18.456 --> 00:12:24.000
and get ahead of the downsides of what we&nbsp;build, and that's responsible development. 

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:30.485
And we&nbsp;certainly engaged in that. And talking with Tim&nbsp;May and putting on that

00:12:30.485 --> 00:12:34.720
 "What if I was a black&nbsp;hat" kind of thing was certainly a useful foil

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:40.960
for working through those kinds of ideas, but that's absolutely not what cypherpunks were.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:43.360
So the progress of the spread of democracy,

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:56.000
if you take a sufficiently long-term view&nbsp;of it, has been really quite remarkable.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.798
Our dream was to enable the&nbsp;future of human freedom,&nbsp;

00:12:59.798 --> 00:13:05.663
and we had this bizarre confidence about how the future would unfold,

00:13:05.663 --> 00:13:14.080
and to use Alan Kay's famous phrase, have a huge hand in inventing it.

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:19.600
But the road had a lot more weeds and&nbsp;detours than we could have anticipated.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:23.675
When we first launched, we were&nbsp;hoping for maybe 400, 500 people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.675 --> 00:13:26.560
Now we're at 100 thousand people.&nbsp;So who knows where we're going next?

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:31.440
Using the ever-growing storage capacity of&nbsp;servers belonging to companies like Google.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:36.990
Instead of it being on your server under your&nbsp;desk, that stuff is being taken of for you.

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:43.702
Professionals keep track of&nbsp;all the programs and the data. Who doesn't want a simpler life? I&nbsp;find this way of working more simple.

00:13:43.702 --> 00:13:48.080
You collect half a gigabyte of information on your&nbsp;customers. What do you do with that information?

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:52.190
They want to know everything about you&nbsp;so they can sell your stuff. 

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:59.701
And it results&nbsp;in you each getting a digital proctology exam. The NSA specifically targets&nbsp;the communications of everyone.

00:13:59.701 --> 00:14:03.440
Over the next quarter-century, the internet&nbsp;would make possible an explosion

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:08.927
of individual freedom and information sharing,&nbsp;just as Phil Salin had predicted in 1991.

00:14:08.927 --> 00:14:13.360
But it would also grow into a surveillance&nbsp;apparatus that bore out the dystopian vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:18.480
of journalist David Burnham in his 1983&nbsp;book, The Rise of the Computer State.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:24.480
"Large bureaucracies with the power that the computer gives them become more powerful

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:30.514
and they are escaping the checks and&nbsp;balances of representative democracy."

00:14:30.514 --> 00:14:35.120
Facebook collects information that the&nbsp;East German Stasi would have killed for.

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:39.680
I think that most people are quite happy to hand&nbsp;people all of the information about them online

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:44.795
in return for a few pics of your high&nbsp;school friends, kids, or whatever.

00:14:44.795 --> 00:14:48.917
By the mid-2000s, it seemed the cypherpunk&nbsp;movement had mostly failed.

00:14:48.917 --> 00:14:54.000
Then came a global financial crisis followed by massive&nbsp;bailouts by central banks.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:00.000
On October 31st, 2008, a pseudonymous inventor named Satoshi&nbsp;Nakamoto shared a white paper

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:06.512
describing a new peer-to-peer, non-governmental monetary system,&nbsp;pulling together technical and philosophical concepts

00:15:06.512 --> 00:15:09.084
developed on the Cypherpunks' email list.

00:15:09.084 --> 00:15:12.577
Within a few years, the movement was reborn with a new generation

00:15:12.577 --> 00:15:17.257
committed to enhancing personal freedom and privacy with cryptographic tools.

00:15:17.257 --> 00:15:20.640
It's like discovering an oasis&nbsp;when you're lost in the desert.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:28.800
It's bitcoin that's single-handedly responsible&nbsp;for the current wave of cypherpunk activity.

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:35.818
It's always messier than visionaries can&nbsp;anticipate because reality is bigger than any one head.

00:15:35.818 --> 00:15:42.340
We're still on the road in the&nbsp;quest to build architectures that amplify&nbsp;human freedoms 

00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:46.543
and protect us from&nbsp;the dynamics in the other direction.

